1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,911
Confidential Mafia is social deduction played on Base, where the chain itself cannot spoil the
secret.

2
00:00:06,011 --> 00:00:15,847
Mafia only works if nobody knows who the Mafia is. On a public blockchain, that's the hard part.
Every transaction is visible, so a naive on-chain Mafia leaks the whole game.

3
00:00:15,947 --> 00:00:23,204
We solve that with Inco Lightning. Players join a lobby with their wallet, and roles are dealt
as encrypted values that live on Inco.

4
00:00:23,304 --> 00:00:33,035
Each player can decrypt exactly one thing: their own role. Inco's per-handle access control
enforces it, so no server, no operator, and no other player can read it.

5
00:00:33,135 --> 00:00:42,390
Here the app fetches this player's encrypted role handle from the contract, and runs an attested
decrypt signed by the player's own key. The role appears only in this browser.

6
00:00:42,490 --> 00:00:49,795
Now the core idea. At night, every living player sends the exact same transaction: submit night
action, with a target.

7
00:00:49,895 --> 00:00:58,139
Nothing on chain distinguishes a Mafia kill from a Doctor save from a Villager doing nothing at
all. The contract works it out entirely under encryption.

8
00:00:58,239 --> 00:01:09,630
It compares the encrypted role against the Mafia and Doctor thresholds, then uses encrypted
select to add a kill weight, or to raise a protection flag. No branch ever reveals which one
happened.

9
00:01:09,730 --> 00:01:18,277
Resolution folds every target with an encrypted running maximum, and computes whether the victim
dies, accounting for the Doctor's protection, still fully encrypted.

10
00:01:18,377 --> 00:01:29,966
Only two values are ever revealed per night: which player was hit, and whether anyone actually
died. Those come back as a covalidator-signed attestation, verified on chain before the contract
will accept them.

11
00:01:30,066 --> 00:01:38,612
A player's role becomes public only when they die. Everyone still alive stays encrypted, which
is what lets the game decide a winner without exposing the survivors.

12
00:01:38,712 --> 00:01:46,086
By day, voting is deliberately public, because arguing about a visible vote is the fun part. The
night is what has to stay secret.

13
00:01:46,186 --> 00:01:56,289
A Gemini narrator posts the story to the Telegram group between phases. It receives a public-
events-only type, so it is structurally incapable of leaking a role it was never given.

14
00:01:56,389 --> 00:02:08,581
Gemini also plays. These are real AI players with their own wallets, and they run under the same
constraint as humans: each can decrypt only its own role, and reasons about everyone else from
public state alone.

15
00:02:08,681 --> 00:02:14,023
That means a game can start even without a full lobby, and the AI is never privileged over you.

16
00:02:14,123 --> 00:02:24,667
Everything you just saw is live on Base Sepolia, in a Telegram Mini App, with real Inco
attestations. Hidden roles, hidden night actions, and an AI that doesn't know the answer either.
